// Mock exam pool, set 1. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"m1-001", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["S3 security","IAM policies"],
 q:"A healthcare analytics company keeps its data lake in a single S3 bucket. More than 40 application teams and partner integrations each need different access to their own prefixes, and some may connect only from specific VPCs. The bucket policy has grown to thousands of lines, is close to its size limit, and every edit risks breaking another team's access. The security team wants to manage permissions per application while keeping the bucket policy short and stable. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Split the data into one bucket per team and copy the shared datasets between the buckets with S3 Same-Region Replication",
    "Replace the bucket policy statements with an IAM group for each team whose identity policies list the allowed prefixes",
    "Create an S3 access point per application with its own access point policy, and delegate access control to the access points",
    "Set S3 Object Ownership to bucket owner preferred and grant each team access to its prefix with object ACLs"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Separate buckets with replication duplicate storage, add replication rules to maintain, and let shared datasets drift between copies; this is far more overhead than access points.",
    "Identity policies work only for principals in the company's own account; partner integrations in other accounts still need bucket policy statements, so the policy does not shrink.",
    "Each access point has its own name, policy, and optional VPC-only network origin, so permissions are managed per application, while the bucket policy only delegates access to access points owned by the account.",
    "ACLs are a legacy mechanism that AWS recommends disabling, they apply per object rather than per prefix, and they cannot restrict access to specific VPCs."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"m1-002", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["SQS","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A video-sharing platform transcodes user uploads on a fleet of EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. Uploads arrive in unpredictable bursts: sometimes almost none for an hour, then thousands within a few minutes. Today the web tier sends each job directly to a transcoding instance over HTTP, and jobs are lost when the fleet is overwhelmed. Each job takes 2–8 minutes. The company requires that no jobs are lost and that the fleet size follows the amount of pending work. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Configure a target tracking scaling policy that keeps the transcoding group's average CPU utilization at 50%",
    "Have the web tier send a message for each upload to an Amazon SQS queue that the transcoding instances poll for work",
    "Place an Application Load Balancer in front of the transcoding instances and enable deregistration delay on the target group",
    "Scale the Auto Scaling group with target tracking on a backlog-per-instance metric calculated from the queue's visible message count",
    "Publish each upload to an Amazon SNS topic and subscribe each transcoding instance to the topic through an HTTP endpoint"],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["CPU is already pegged whenever the fleet is busy and says nothing about how many jobs are waiting, so a growing backlog would not drive enough scale-out.",
    "SQS durably buffers every job (for up to 14 days) until an instance is free to process it, so bursts no longer cause lost work and the web tier is decoupled from the fleet.",
    "A load balancer still forwards requests synchronously; when every instance is saturated, requests fail and jobs are lost. Deregistration delay only lets in-flight requests finish on instances that are leaving.",
    "Dividing <code>ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible</code> by the number of running instances gives a backlog-per-instance metric; target tracking on it adds instances as work piles up and removes them as the queue drains.",
    "SNS pushes each message to every subscriber, so all instances would receive every job, and HTTP delivery retries are limited, so jobs can still be lost while instances are overwhelmed."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9b1","d5c2"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"m1-003", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["EBS & instance store"],
 q:"A company is migrating a clustered Linux application that uses a cluster-aware shared-disk file system, such as GFS2, to Amazon EC2. Three instances in the same Availability Zone must have concurrent read and write access to the same block device with low, consistent latency, and the application cannot be changed to use a network file system. Which storage solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["An Amazon EFS file system mounted on all three instances in the General Purpose performance mode",
    "An io2 Block Express volume with Multi-Attach enabled, attached to all three Nitro-based instances",
    "A gp3 volume on one instance that is shared with the other two instances over NFS from that instance",
    "Instance store NVMe volumes on each instance, kept in sync with a nightly rsync between the instances"],
 a:[1],
 x:["EFS is a network file system accessed over NFS, which the application cannot use, and it does not present a shared block device.",
    "Provisioned IOPS io1 and io2 volumes support Multi-Attach to multiple Nitro-based instances in the same AZ, and the cluster-aware file system coordinates their concurrent writes.",
    "Sharing a volume over NFS from one instance creates a single point of failure and is still a network file system, not a shared block device.",
    "Instance store volumes are local to each host and cannot be shared, and a nightly sync does not give concurrent access to the same data."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"m1-004", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A company runs an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for application logs and keeps 12 months of indices on hot data nodes with SSD storage. Almost all searches cover the last 7 days. Older indices are never updated and are searched only a few times a month during investigations, when slower queries are acceptable. Hot storage is now the largest cost of the domain. Which solution reduces cost the MOST while keeping all 12 months searchable?",
 o:["Add more hot data nodes with larger SSD volumes so that the existing indices are spread more evenly",
    "Reduce the number of replica shards for every index to zero to halve the storage used on the hot nodes",
    "Delete indices older than 7 days and restore them from manual snapshots when an investigation starts",
    "Use Index State Management to move indices older than 7 days from the hot nodes to UltraWarm storage"],
 a:[3],
 x:["More hot nodes increase cost, and spreading indices does not reduce the amount of expensive hot storage.",
    "Removing replicas saves some storage but risks data loss and unavailability when a node fails, and all data still sits on costly hot storage.",
    "Deleted indices are no longer searchable, and restoring snapshots for every investigation is slow manual work, so the requirement is not met.",
    "UltraWarm keeps read-only indices searchable on S3-backed storage at a much lower cost per GB than hot nodes, and Index State Management moves indices there automatically as they age."],
 ref:["d11e4","d10c3"]},

{id:"m1-005", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Secrets Manager & Parameter Store","IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A shared-services account stores a database credential in AWS Secrets Manager, encrypted with the default AWS managed key (aws/secretsmanager). A Lambda function in a separate application account must read the secret at runtime. The security team wants the secret to stay only in the shared-services account and access to be limited to that function's execution role, with no long-term credentials. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a resource policy to the secret that allows the role, and keep the secret encrypted with the AWS managed key",
    "Encrypt the secret with a customer managed key, and allow the role in both the secret's resource policy and the key policy",
    "Replicate the secret to the application account's Region with Secrets Manager replication, and have the function read the replica",
    "Create an IAM user in the shared-services account and store its access keys in the function's environment variables"],
 a:[1],
 x:["The key policy of an AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow another account, so the role could not decrypt the secret even with the resource policy in place.",
    "Cross-account access needs a resource policy on the secret, a customer managed key whose key policy lets the role decrypt, and an identity policy on the role that allows both actions.",
    "Secrets Manager replication copies a secret to other Regions in the same account, so it does not make the secret available to a different account.",
    "Access keys are long-term credentials, which the security team forbids, and environment variables are a poor place to keep them."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4b5","d8c2"], doc:"secrets"},

{id:"m1-006", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Aurora","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"An insurance company runs its policy administration system on Amazon Aurora MySQL in us-east-1. For a full Regional outage, the business requires an RPO measured in seconds and an RTO of only a few minutes. The company also wants underwriters in Europe to run read-only reports against a local copy of the data with low latency. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Use AWS Backup to copy hourly Aurora snapshots to eu-west-1 and restore a new cluster from the latest copy during a disaster",
    "Add two Aurora Replicas in different Availability Zones of us-east-1 and rely on automatic failover to one of them",
    "Convert the cluster to an Aurora global database with a secondary cluster in eu-west-1 used for local reads and Regional failover",
    "Create a cross-Region read replica in eu-west-1 by using MySQL binary log replication, and promote it during a disaster"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Hourly snapshot copies give an RPO of up to an hour, and restoring a new cluster takes far longer than a few minutes.",
    "Aurora Replicas in the same Region protect against instance or Availability Zone failure, not the loss of the whole Region, and they give Europe no local copy.",
    "Aurora Global Database replicates at the storage layer with typical lag under a second, the secondary cluster serves local reads, and a managed failover promotes the secondary Region within minutes.",
    "Binlog replication runs in the database engine, so lag grows under heavy write load and promotion takes longer, which makes a seconds-level RPO and minutes-level RTO unreliable; Global Database is the purpose-built option."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c4"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"m1-007", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Cognito","API Gateway"],
 q:"A fitness startup is building a mobile app and a single-page web app that call Amazon API Gateway REST APIs. Customers must be able to sign up with an email address and password or sign in with their Google accounts, and the company wants MFA, password reset, and account recovery without writing its own authentication code. API Gateway must reject any request that does not include a valid token from a signed-in customer. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an Amazon Cognito identity pool that federates with Google, and give authenticated identities an IAM role that allows <code>execute-api:Invoke</code>",
    "Enable IAM Identity Center with Google as the external identity provider, and use IAM authorization on the API methods",
    "Store customer credentials in an Amazon DynamoDB table and write a Lambda authorizer that verifies passwords and issues JWTs",
    "Create an Amazon Cognito user pool with Google as a social identity provider, and add a Cognito user pool authorizer to the API methods"],
 a:[3],
 x:["An identity pool exchanges identities for temporary AWS credentials but is not a user directory; on its own it provides no email sign-up, MFA, or password reset.",
    "IAM Identity Center is for workforce access to AWS accounts and business applications, not for members of the public signing up to a consumer app.",
    "This works but means building and securing password storage, MFA, and token issuance yourself, which is far more operational overhead than a managed service.",
    "A user pool provides sign-up, sign-in, MFA, account recovery, and Google federation as a managed service, and the user pool authorizer validates its tokens in API Gateway with no custom code."],
 ref:["d8e3","d8c1"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"m1-008", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["RDS","Lambda"],
 q:"A ride-sharing startup's API runs on AWS Lambda and queries an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. During evening peaks, thousands of concurrent invocations each open a new database connection, the database reaches its max_connections limit, and requests fail with 'too many connections' errors even though database CPU stays around 40%. The team must fix the errors with minimal code changes and without throttling customer requests. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create an Amazon RDS Proxy for the database and change the functions' connection string to the proxy endpoint",
    "Move the DB instance to a larger instance class so that its max_connections limit is raised automatically",
    "Set reserved concurrency on the Lambda functions low enough to keep connections below max_connections",
    "Add two RDS read replicas and distribute the functions' queries evenly across the replica endpoints"],
 a:[0],
 x:["RDS Proxy pools and shares a small number of database connections across thousands of Lambda invocations, absorbing connection storms with only an endpoint change in the code.",
    "A larger class raises the memory-based connection default but wastes money when CPU is only at 40%, and a bigger spike can still exhaust connections.",
    "Capping concurrency avoids the database errors by throttling invocations, which directly violates the requirement not to throttle customer requests.",
    "Replicas help only read queries, require code to split reads from writes, and each replica would still face its own connection storm."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"m1-009", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A company runs a steady, around-the-clock compute baseline on EC2 instances in eu-west-1. Over the next year it plans to move some services to AWS Fargate and AWS Lambda, migrate part of the remaining fleet to us-east-1, and switch instance families as it adopts Graviton. The company wants a 3-year commitment for the biggest discount it can get while keeping all of this flexibility. Which purchasing option should it choose?",
 o:["EC2 Instance Savings Plans for the instance family the company currently uses in eu-west-1",
    "Standard Reserved Instances for the current instance types, resold on the Reserved Instance Marketplace later",
    "A Compute Savings Plan sized to the company's steady hourly compute spend",
    "Spot Instances for the baseline, with On-Demand capacity configured as a fallback"],
 a:[2],
 x:["EC2 Instance Savings Plans give a deeper discount but are locked to one instance family in one Region and do not apply to Fargate or Lambda.",
    "Standard RIs are tied to an instance family and Region and never cover Fargate or Lambda; relying on resale to escape the commitment is not flexibility.",
    "Compute Savings Plans apply automatically across instance families, sizes, Regions, operating systems, Fargate, and Lambda, so the discount follows the workload as it changes.",
    "Spot capacity can be interrupted at any time, so it is unsuitable for a steady baseline that must run continuously, and it involves no commitment-based discount."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2","d1c7"], doc:"ec2-pricing"},

{id:"m1-010", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["SNS","SQS"],
 q:"An e-commerce company publishes an event each time an order is placed. Billing, warehouse, and fraud-scoring services must each process every order event independently and at their own pace. If one service is offline for several hours for maintenance, it must catch up on the events it missed without affecting the others. More consumers will be added next year without any change to the publisher. Which design meets these requirements?",
 o:["Publish each order event to a single Amazon SQS standard queue that all three services poll for messages",
    "Publish each order event to an Amazon SNS topic and subscribe a separate Amazon SQS queue for each service",
    "Publish each order event to an Amazon SNS topic and subscribe each service directly through an HTTPS endpoint",
    "Have the publisher write a copy of each order event to a dedicated Amazon SQS queue for each service"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Consumers of one queue compete for messages, so each event would be processed by only one of the three services instead of all of them.",
    "SNS fans each event out to every subscribed queue, and each queue holds its copy until that service is ready, so an offline service catches up alone; new queues can subscribe without touching the publisher.",
    "SNS retries HTTPS deliveries only for a limited time, so a service that is offline for hours would lose events unless extra dead-letter handling were built.",
    "This works for the current three services, but the publisher must change every time a consumer is added, which breaks the decoupling requirement."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9b2"], doc:"sns"},

{id:"m1-011", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Organizations & SCPs","CloudTrail"],
 q:"A company uses AWS Organizations with all features enabled. The security team must ensure that no principal in any member account, including account administrators and the root user, can create resources outside eu-west-1 and eu-central-1, and that no one in a member account can stop, delete, or modify the CloudTrail trails the security team deploys. Global services such as IAM must keep working. The controls must apply automatically to accounts added later. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Attach an SCP to the organization root that denies actions when <code>aws:RequestedRegion</code> is not one of the two approved Regions, exempting global services",
    "Create an IAM permissions boundary in every member account that denies actions outside the approved Regions, and attach it to all roles",
    "Deploy AWS Config rules in every account that flag resources created outside the approved Regions and notify the security team",
    "Attach an SCP to the organization root that denies <code>cloudtrail:StopLogging</code>, <code>cloudtrail:DeleteTrail</code>, and <code>cloudtrail:UpdateTrail</code>",
    "Add a policy that denies the CloudTrail stop, delete, and update actions to every permission set in IAM Identity Center"],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["An SCP is a preventive guardrail that applies to every principal in member accounts, including administrators and the root user, and is inherited by new accounts; exempting global services (for example with NotAction) keeps IAM working.",
    "Boundaries must be attached to each user or role individually, do not restrict the root user or accounts created later, and an account administrator could detach them.",
    "Config rules detect noncompliant resources after they are created; they do not prevent anyone from launching resources in other Regions.",
    "Because SCPs cap the permissions of every principal in member accounts, no one there can disable or change the trails, whatever their IAM policies allow; a condition can exempt the security team's deployment role.",
    "Permission set policies affect only users who sign in through IAM Identity Center; IAM users and roles created locally in accounts, and the root user, would not be restricted."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c2"], doc:"organizations"},

{id:"m1-012", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["CloudFront"],
 q:"A news website serves readers worldwide from eu-west-1. Images, CSS, and JavaScript are stored in an S3 bucket, and personalized article pages and JSON APIs come from EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. Readers in Asia and the Americas report slow page loads. The company wants lower latency for both the static assets and the personalized responses and less load on the origin, and personalized pages must never be served to the wrong reader. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and change the site to load images and scripts from the accelerate endpoint",
    "Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator with the S3 bucket and the ALB as endpoints so traffic uses the AWS global network",
    "Create a CloudFront distribution with the S3 bucket as an origin using origin access control, and a cache behavior with long TTLs for static paths",
    "Cache every path, including personalized pages, in CloudFront with a 24-hour TTL and a cache key based only on the URL path",
    "Add the ALB as a second origin with a cache behavior for dynamic paths that disables caching and forwards the needed cookies and headers"],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers to and from the bucket but does no caching, so it neither offloads the origin nor helps the ALB-served pages.",
    "Global Accelerator cannot use an S3 bucket as an endpoint and does not cache content, so every static asset would still be fetched from eu-west-1.",
    "Edge caching with long TTLs serves static assets from locations near readers and removes most of that traffic from the origin, while OAC keeps the bucket private.",
    "A cache key based only on the path would serve one reader's personalized page to other readers, which violates the requirement.",
    "Dynamic requests still benefit from CloudFront because connections terminate at a nearby edge and reach the origin over the AWS backbone on persistent connections, while disabling caching keeps responses per reader."],
 ref:["d10e5","d10b1","d10c1"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"m1-013", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["S3 features","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A media company stores user uploads in S3 buckets in us-east-1 and us-west-2 that are kept in sync with two-way S3 replication. Applications in several Regions must use a single global endpoint for all S3 reads and writes. Normally all requests must go to us-east-1, and if us-east-1 has a problem the operations team must be able to shift requests to us-west-2 within minutes without changing application configuration or DNS records. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create Route 53 failover records that point to the two buckets' Regional endpoints and have the applications use the record name",
    "Put a CloudFront distribution with an origin group in front of both buckets and send all reads and writes through it",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on both buckets and have the applications switch to the other accelerate endpoint during an outage",
    "Create an S3 Multi-Region Access Point for both buckets in an active-passive configuration and use its failover controls"],
 a:[3],
 x:["S3 answers a custom host name only when the bucket is named after it, and two buckets cannot share a name, so DNS failover between them does not work for S3 API requests.",
    "CloudFront origin groups fail over only GET, HEAD, and OPTIONS requests, so writes would not be redirected during an outage.",
    "Switching to the other bucket's endpoint means changing application configuration, which the requirement rules out; Transfer Acceleration only speeds up long-distance transfers.",
    "A Multi-Region Access Point gives one global endpoint for the replicated buckets; in active-passive mode all requests go to the active Region, and failover controls shift traffic to the passive Region within minutes."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"m1-014", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["S3 security","VPC endpoints & PrivateLink"],
 q:"A research institute stores genomic datasets in an S3 bucket. Analysis runs on EC2 instances in a single VPC that reaches S3 through a gateway VPC endpoint. A compliance rule states that objects in the bucket may be read only through that specific endpoint, even by IAM principals whose identity policies allow <code>s3:*</code>, so that the data cannot be read from the internet, from on-premises networks, or from other VPCs. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Add a bucket policy statement that denies <code>s3:GetObject</code> unless <code>aws:SourceVpce</code> equals the ID of the gateway endpoint",
    "Add a bucket policy statement that allows <code>s3:GetObject</code> only when <code>aws:SourceIp</code> is within the VPC's CIDR range",
    "Attach a VPC endpoint policy to the gateway endpoint that allows access to this bucket and denies all other buckets",
    "Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the bucket and the account, and disable access control lists on the bucket"],
 a:[0],
 x:["An explicit deny in the bucket policy overrides any identity-based allow, so requests that do not arrive through the named endpoint are refused for every principal.",
    "For requests through a VPC endpoint, <code>aws:SourceIp</code> does not carry the instance's private address (<code>aws:VpcSourceIp</code> does), and an extra allow cannot override the allows that principals with <code>s3:*</code> already have.",
    "An endpoint policy limits what can be reached through the endpoint; it does nothing to stop the bucket being read from outside the VPC by other paths.",
    "Block Public Access stops anonymous and public grants, but authenticated IAM principals with <code>s3:*</code> could still read objects from anywhere."],
 ref:["d8e4","d2c3","d8b1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"m1-015", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"A machine learning platform stores 800 TB of training datasets in S3 Standard. Each dataset may be used heavily for a few weeks, ignored for months, and then needed again without warning, and the patterns differ by dataset and cannot be predicted. When a dataset is needed, it must be readable within milliseconds. The team wants to lower storage costs without paying per-GB retrieval fees and without building access-analysis tooling. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that transitions objects to S3 Standard-IA 30 days after creation",
    "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that transitions objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days",
    "Transition the datasets to S3 Intelligent-Tiering and use its default automatic access tiers",
    "Move the datasets to S3 One Zone-IA and keep a second copy in another Region for resilience"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Standard-IA charges a per-GB retrieval fee, and transitioning on age alone moves datasets that may still be busy, so unpredictable re-reads become expensive.",
    "Glacier Flexible Retrieval needs minutes to hours to restore objects, which fails the millisecond-access requirement.",
    "Intelligent-Tiering moves each object between frequent, infrequent, and archive instant access tiers based on its own access, with millisecond reads and no retrieval fees, only a small per-object monitoring charge.",
    "One Zone-IA still charges retrieval fees, and keeping a second copy in another Region adds storage and replication costs that offset the savings."],
 ref:["d11e2","d11c1"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"m1-016", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["API Gateway","Lambda"],
 q:"A sports app shows live match scores. Hundreds of thousands of mobile clients poll a REST API in Amazon API Gateway every two seconds, and most responses are unchanged, which drives up Lambda invocations and backend load. The company wants the backend to push each score change to connected clients within a second, using a serverless architecture with no servers to manage. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable API Gateway caching on the REST API stage with a two-second TTL so that repeated polls are served from the cache",
    "Create an API Gateway WebSocket API, store connection IDs in DynamoDB, and have Lambda post updates to connected clients",
    "Put a CloudFront distribution in front of the REST API and cache the scores endpoint at edge locations for one second",
    "Run EC2 instances behind a Network Load Balancer that hold long-lived TCP connections open to every mobile client"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Caching lowers backend load for repeated polls, but clients still poll constantly and see changes only after the TTL expires; nothing is pushed to them.",
    "A WebSocket API keeps a persistent connection per client; Lambda stores connection IDs when clients connect and calls the @connections management API to push each update as soon as a score changes, with no servers to manage.",
    "Edge caching reduces origin requests, but clients still poll and receive changes only after the cache expires; it is not a push model.",
    "An NLB with EC2 instances can hold connections, but the company would have to manage servers and connection state, which violates the serverless requirement."],
 ref:["d10e3","d10b2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"m1-017", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","Load balancing"],
 q:"A payment company's public API runs behind an Application Load Balancer with an HTTPS listener. A compliance scan reports that the listener still accepts TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 connections and weak cipher suites. Auditors require that only TLS 1.2 or later with strong ciphers be negotiated with clients, with no changes to the application or its servers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add an AWS WAF rule to the ALB that blocks requests whose TLS protocol version is lower than TLS 1.2",
    "Replace the ACM certificate on the listener with a new certificate that uses a 4096-bit RSA key",
    "Change the HTTPS listener's security policy to a predefined policy that allows only TLS 1.2 and later",
    "Move TLS termination to the EC2 instances and configure TLS 1.2 or later in the web server software"],
 a:[2],
 x:["WAF inspects HTTP requests after the TLS handshake has completed, so it cannot stop clients from negotiating old protocol versions.",
    "The certificate's key size does not control which protocol versions and cipher suites the listener negotiates.",
    "An ALB security policy defines the protocols and ciphers that the listener accepts, so choosing a predefined policy for TLS 1.2 and later (or TLS 1.3) removes TLS 1.0, TLS 1.1, and weak ciphers without touching the application.",
    "Terminating TLS on the instances means managing certificates and TLS settings on every server, which changes the servers and adds work."],
 ref:["d8e3","d8c3","d5b3"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"m1-018", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A trading platform sends account transactions such as deposits, withdrawals, and trades to a processing service through a message queue. Transactions for the same account must be processed in exactly the order they were submitted, and a transaction must not be processed twice when a producer retries a send after a network timeout. Transactions for different accounts must be processed in parallel so the service keeps up with volume. Which solution meets these requirements with the least custom code?",
 o:["An Amazon SQS FIFO queue, using the account ID as the message group ID and a unique deduplication ID for each transaction",
    "An Amazon SQS standard queue with a sequence number in each message, and consumers that reorder and deduplicate in memory",
    "An Amazon SQS FIFO queue, using one fixed message group ID for every transaction and content-based deduplication",
    "An Amazon SNS standard topic that delivers to an Amazon SQS standard queue with raw message delivery enabled"],
 a:[0],
 x:["FIFO queues keep strict order within each message group, process different groups in parallel, and discard producer retries that reuse a deduplication ID within the 5-minute deduplication interval.",
    "Standard queues offer best-effort ordering and at-least-once delivery, so the consumers would need complex custom reordering and deduplication logic.",
    "A single message group preserves order and deduplication but forces every transaction through one ordered stream, so different accounts cannot be processed in parallel.",
    "Standard topics and queues give no ordering or deduplication guarantees, so transactions could arrive out of order or more than once."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"m1-019", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"A food-delivery app stores orders in an Amazon DynamoDB table that uses on-demand capacity mode. Traffic is highly predictable: a steady base load all day that ramps smoothly to about three times the base at lunch and dinner. The finance team notes that the table costs much more than expected for such a steady workload. The table must keep handling the daily peaks without throttling. Which change is the most cost-effective?",
 o:["Keep on-demand capacity mode and add a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster in front of the table",
    "Switch the table to provisioned capacity mode with auto scaling that targets a utilization percentage",
    "Switch the table to provisioned capacity mode with fixed capacity sized for the dinner peak all day",
    "Change the table to the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access table class to lower its overall costs"],
 a:[1],
 x:["DAX only offloads repeated reads and adds node costs; order writes would still be billed at on-demand rates.",
    "For smooth, predictable traffic, provisioned capacity that auto scaling raises and lowers around the daily ramps costs less than paying per request, without throttling at the peaks.",
    "Fixed peak capacity avoids throttling but pays for three times the base load around the clock, wasting most of it.",
    "Standard-IA lowers storage cost but raises the price of reads and writes, so it is the wrong lever for a throughput-heavy order table."],
 ref:["d11e4","d3b3"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"m1-020", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","CloudFront"],
 q:"A retailer hosts its storefront in eu-west-1 behind an Application Load Balancer and is adding an Amazon CloudFront distribution that uses the custom domain shop.example.com. Security requires HTTPS from viewers to CloudFront and from CloudFront to the origin, using public certificates that renew automatically and whose private keys are never handled by staff. A public ACM certificate for shop.example.com was requested in eu-west-1 and attached to the ALB, but it cannot be selected for the distribution. What should the solutions architect do?",
 o:["Export the eu-west-1 certificate and its private key from ACM, and upload them to the IAM certificate store for CloudFront",
    "Set the distribution's origin protocol policy to HTTPS only so that CloudFront presents the ALB's certificate to viewers",
    "Request a public ACM certificate for shop.example.com in us-east-1 and associate it with the CloudFront distribution",
    "Issue a certificate for shop.example.com from AWS Private CA in eu-west-1 and associate it with the distribution"],
 a:[2],
 x:["ACM can export only certificates that were issued as exportable, and handling the private key by hand is exactly what the requirement forbids; certificates in the IAM store are also not renewed automatically.",
    "The origin protocol policy only secures the CloudFront-to-ALB connection; viewers still need a certificate for shop.example.com attached to the distribution itself.",
    "CloudFront uses ACM certificates only from us-east-1 (N. Virginia); a public ACM certificate there renews automatically and its private key is never exposed to staff.",
    "Browsers do not trust certificates issued by a private CA, so public viewers would get certificate errors."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3"], doc:"acm"},

{id:"m1-021", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Caching"],
 q:"A gaming company keeps player state in hashes and sorted sets in Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey with cluster mode disabled. The cluster has one primary node of the largest available node type and no replicas. During tournaments the primary node's CPU is saturated by a mix of writes and reads, and its memory is nearly full. The cache must scale beyond a single node for both writes and reads. Which combination of actions will meet these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable cluster mode and spread the keyspace across multiple shards, each with its own primary node",
    "Enable Multi-AZ with automatic failover so that a standby node can take over if the primary node fails",
    "Increase the TTL of every cached key so that the application writes to the cache less often",
    "Add read replicas to each shard and configure the client to send read commands to the replicas",
    "Move to an ElastiCache for Memcached cluster with the same node type and keep the same data structures"],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["With cluster mode enabled, data is partitioned across shards and each shard has its own primary, so write throughput and memory capacity grow with the number of shards.",
    "Multi-AZ automatic failover improves availability only; it adds no write capacity or memory and does not by itself move read traffic off the primary.",
    "The writes come from game state changes, not from cache misses, so a longer TTL does not reduce write load and makes the memory pressure worse.",
    "Replicas can serve read commands, so sending reads to them takes read load off each shard's primary and scales read throughput.",
    "Memcached stores only simple string values, so it cannot hold the hashes and sorted sets, and it has no replication."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"m1-022", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A logistics company runs a customer-facing tracking application on EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer, with an Amazon RDS for MySQL database, in eu-west-1. For a Regional disaster the business requires an RTO of 10 minutes and an RPO of under 1 minute. Building and configuring the application tier from scratch in another Region takes about 45 minutes. The company does not want to pay for a full second production environment. Which disaster recovery strategy meets these requirements most cost-effectively?",
 o:["Pilot light, with a cross-Region read replica in a second Region and the application tier deployed from templates only during a disaster",
    "Backup and restore, with snapshots and AMIs copied to a second Region by AWS Backup and restored during a disaster",
    "Multi-site active/active, with full production capacity in two Regions behind Route 53 latency-based routing",
    "Warm standby, with a scaled-down but running copy of the stack and a cross-Region read replica that are scaled up and promoted"],
 a:[3],
 x:["The data would be protected, but launching and configuring the application tier takes about 45 minutes, which breaks the 10-minute RTO.",
    "Restoring databases and servers from backups takes hours and backups are periodic, so neither the RTO nor the RPO would be met.",
    "Active/active meets the objectives but pays for a full second production environment, which the company wants to avoid.",
    "A small, always-running environment only needs to scale out and promote the replica, which fits a 10-minute RTO, while asynchronous replication keeps the RPO within seconds at far less than the cost of a full copy."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"]},

{id:"m1-023", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager","Load balancing"],
 q:"An online ticketing company runs its booking site on EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. During popular on-sales, a few hundred IP addresses send thousands of requests per minute to the /login path in a credential-stuffing attack, slowing the site for genuine customers. The company wants any single IP address that exceeds a request threshold on /login to be blocked automatically for as long as it stays above the threshold, with no application code changes and no effect on normal customers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add deny rules for the attacking IP addresses to the network ACLs of the ALB subnets and update them during each on-sale",
    "Associate an AWS WAF web ACL with the ALB that has a rate-based rule scoped down to requests whose URI path starts with /login",
    "Enable AWS Shield Advanced on the ALB so that volumetric network-layer attacks are detected and absorbed automatically",
    "Add security group rules to the ALB that limit how many concurrent connections each source IP address can open"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Network ACLs are updated by hand, have a small rule limit, and cannot react automatically to new attacking addresses.",
    "A rate-based rule counts requests per IP address over a rolling window and blocks addresses while they exceed the limit, and the scope-down statement applies it only to /login traffic.",
    "Shield Advanced targets DDoS events; a credential-stuffing attack from a few hundred addresses is low-volume application traffic that needs a per-IP request limit on one path.",
    "Security groups only allow traffic by protocol, port, and source; they cannot count or limit requests or connections per IP address."],
 ref:["d8e3","d8c3","d4c3"], doc:"waf"},

{id:"m1-024", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["EBS & instance store"],
 q:"A company has 400 TB of Amazon EBS gp2 volumes attached to production EC2 instances. Many volumes were created larger than needed only because gp2 IOPS scale with volume size. The company wants to lower its EBS bill without downtime and without reducing the IOPS or throughput that the applications currently get. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Take snapshots of the volumes and restore them as Throughput Optimized HDD (st1) volumes of the same size",
    "Use Elastic Volumes to change the volumes to io2 so that their IOPS are provisioned and guaranteed",
    "Use Elastic Volumes to change the volumes to gp3 in place, provisioning IOPS and throughput at current levels",
    "Take snapshots and restore them as smaller gp2 volumes that match the amount of data actually stored"],
 a:[2],
 x:["st1 is designed for large sequential workloads and delivers poor random IOPS, and swapping in volumes restored from snapshots requires downtime.",
    "io2 provides provisioned IOPS but costs more per GB and per IOPS than gp3, so the bill would increase.",
    "gp3 costs less per GB than gp2 and lets IOPS and throughput be provisioned independently of size, and Elastic Volumes changes the type while the volume stays attached and in use.",
    "Smaller gp2 volumes get proportionally fewer IOPS, which would lower performance, and the snapshot-and-swap process needs downtime."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"m1-025", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["RDS","High availability design"],
 q:"A SaaS company runs Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL as a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment. In a recent failover test, the application was unavailable for longer than the business accepts, and the reporting team complains that heavy read queries slow down the primary. The company wants failovers that typically complete in under 35 seconds and wants its standby capacity to serve read traffic. It must remain on the RDS for PostgreSQL engine, with application changes limited to connection endpoints. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Keep the Multi-AZ DB instance deployment and point the reporting queries at the standby instance's endpoint",
    "Move to an RDS Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment and send the reporting queries to the cluster's reader endpoint",
    "Add a cross-Region read replica of the database and send the reporting queries to the replica's endpoint",
    "Migrate the database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL and send the reporting queries to an Aurora Replica"],
 a:[1],
 x:["The standby in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment is not readable; it exists only for failover, so there is no endpoint for reporting.",
    "A Multi-AZ DB cluster has a writer and two readable standbys in different Availability Zones, typically fails over in under 35 seconds, and exposes a reader endpoint for read traffic.",
    "A cross-Region replica offloads reads but does nothing to speed up failover of the primary deployment.",
    "Aurora offers fast failover and readable replicas, but it is a different engine, and the company must stay on RDS for PostgreSQL."],
 ref:["d9e3","d3c2","d3p1"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"m1-026", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Migration & transfer","Analytics"],
 q:"A sales operations team wants to analyze Salesforce opportunity and account data together with order data in its Amazon S3 data lake. The Salesforce data must be copied into S3 every hour, incrementally and encrypted, with no custom code, servers, or connectors to build or maintain. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Write a Lambda function that calls the Salesforce REST API every hour, pages through changed records, and writes JSON files to S3",
    "Run an AWS Glue Spark job on an hourly schedule that connects to Salesforce through a custom JDBC driver uploaded to S3",
    "Deploy an AWS DataSync agent near the Salesforce instance and run an hourly task that copies the records to the S3 bucket",
    "Create an Amazon AppFlow flow from Salesforce to the S3 bucket that runs every hour and transfers only new and changed records"],
 a:[3],
 x:["A custom Lambda integration means writing and maintaining API calls, paging, token refresh, and error handling, which the team wants to avoid.",
    "A Spark job with a custom JDBC driver is code and a component to maintain for something AppFlow does natively.",
    "DataSync copies files and objects between storage systems such as NFS, SMB, HDFS, and S3; it cannot extract records from a SaaS application such as Salesforce.",
    "AppFlow is a managed integration service with a built-in Salesforce connector; a scheduled flow can transfer incremental changes to S3, encrypted with KMS, without code or infrastructure."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"]},

{id:"m1-027", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["IAM Identity Center & federation","Hybrid connectivity"],
 q:"A company is moving Windows file servers and line-of-business applications to EC2 instances. The instances must join the company's existing on-premises Active Directory domain, and employees must keep signing in with their current domain accounts. Corporate policy forbids storing or replicating any directory data in AWS, and the company wants the least operational overhead. The data center is connected to the VPC through redundant AWS Direct Connect connections. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Deploy an AD Connector in the VPC that proxies directory requests to the on-premises domain controllers, and use it to join the instances",
    "Deploy AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory in the VPC and create a two-way forest trust with the on-premises domain",
    "Launch additional domain controllers for the on-premises domain on EC2 instances in two Availability Zones and let them replicate",
    "Enable IAM Identity Center with the on-premises Active Directory as its identity source and assign permission sets to the instances"],
 a:[0],
 x:["AD Connector is a directory gateway that forwards requests to the on-premises domain controllers without caching or storing directory data in AWS, and it supports seamless domain join for EC2 Windows instances.",
    "AWS Managed Microsoft AD creates a separate directory whose data lives in AWS, which the policy forbids, and a second forest adds administration.",
    "Extra domain controllers on EC2 replicate the full directory into AWS, which violates the policy, and the company would have to patch and operate them.",
    "IAM Identity Center gives the workforce access to AWS accounts and applications; it does not join EC2 instances to a Windows domain."],
 ref:["d8e2","d8c1"]},

{id:"m1-028", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Step Functions","Lambda"],
 q:"A travel company's booking workflow calls three Lambda functions in sequence: reserve a flight, reserve a hotel, and charge the customer's card. The hotel partner's API sometimes returns transient throttling errors that succeed on a later attempt. If the payment step fails permanently, the flight and hotel reservations that were already made must be cancelled. The company wants to remove the hand-written orchestration code from its application and rely on managed workflow features. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Chain the three functions with Lambda on-success destinations, and send failed invocations to an on-failure destination",
    "Implement the workflow as an AWS Step Functions state machine with a Retry and exponential backoff on the hotel task",
    "Place an Amazon SQS queue with a dead-letter queue between each pair of steps to absorb failures between the functions",
    "Raise the hotel function's timeout to 15 minutes and add a sleep-and-retry loop around the partner API call in its code",
    "Add a Catch to the payment task in the state machine that routes to states that cancel the hotel and flight reservations"],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Destinations pass results from one asynchronous invocation to the next, but there is no central place for retries with backoff or for running compensating cancellations.",
    "Step Functions replaces the hand-written orchestration, and a Retry with exponential backoff on the hotel task handles transient throttling declaratively.",
    "Queues decouple the steps, but a dead-letter queue only parks failed messages; nothing would cancel the earlier reservations when payment fails.",
    "This keeps retry logic in application code and pays for idle waiting inside the function, which is exactly what the company wants to remove.",
    "A Catch on the payment task routes permanent failures to compensating states (the saga pattern) that undo the earlier reservations."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"m1-029", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Governance","Shared responsibility"],
 q:"A payment company is preparing for its annual PCI DSS assessment. The external assessor asks for evidence that the AWS data centers and infrastructure that host the company's cardholder data environment are themselves PCI DSS compliant, including AWS's Attestation of Compliance. The compliance team needs these documents today, without waiting on anyone at AWS. What should the compliance team do?",
 o:["Deploy AWS Config conformance packs for PCI DSS in every account and export the compliance results for the assessor",
    "Enable Security Hub CSPM with the PCI DSS standard and share the resulting security score report with the assessor",
    "Open a case with AWS Support and ask an engineer to email the AWS PCI DSS report directly to the external assessor",
    "Download the AWS PCI DSS Attestation of Compliance and the related reports from AWS Artifact in the console"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Conformance packs check how the company configured its own resources, which is the customer's side of the shared responsibility model; they say nothing about AWS's infrastructure.",
    "Security Hub CSPM evaluates the company's resources against PCI DSS controls, not AWS's own data centers, so its results are not AWS's attestation.",
    "A support case adds delay for no reason, because AWS publishes its compliance reports for self-service download.",
    "AWS Artifact is the self-service portal for AWS's compliance reports, such as SOC reports and the PCI DSS Attestation of Compliance, available on demand."],
 ref:["d6w2","d8e3"]},

{id:"m1-030", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["EC2 purchase options","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A genomics lab runs nightly batch jobs on EC2 instances that each process an independent sample. Jobs write checkpoints to Amazon S3 every few minutes and can resume after an interruption, and results are needed by the next morning. The lab wants to reduce compute cost as much as possible while minimizing the chance that many jobs are interrupted at once because one instance type runs short of capacity. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Run the jobs on Spot Instances in an Auto Scaling group that specifies many similar-sized instance types across multiple Availability Zones",
    "Use the lowest-price Spot allocation strategy with a single instance type chosen for the best price per vCPU",
    "Use the price-capacity-optimized allocation strategy so Spot capacity comes from the deepest, lowest-priced pools",
    "Purchase a 1-year Compute Savings Plan that covers the nightly batch capacity at a committed hourly spend",
    "Run the jobs on On-Demand Instances in a cluster placement group to keep all of the job nodes close together"],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Spot offers the largest discount for fault-tolerant, checkpointed work, and diversifying across instance types and AZs spreads the risk so a shortage in one pool does not stop most jobs.",
    "Concentrating on the single cheapest pool maximizes the chance of simultaneous interruptions when that pool runs low on capacity.",
    "Price-capacity-optimized chooses pools with the most available capacity at low prices, which AWS recommends to reduce interruptions while keeping costs low.",
    "A Savings Plan commits to spend for every hour of the day while the jobs run only at night, and its discount is smaller than Spot for interruptible work.",
    "On-Demand is the most expensive option, and the jobs are independent, so they gain nothing from a cluster placement group."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2"], doc:"ec2-pricing"},

{id:"m1-031", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Caching"],
 q:"An online gaming platform runs its web tier on an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances. Player session data is held in each instance's memory, so players are logged out when instances scale in, and a real-time global leaderboard is recalculated every few seconds with expensive SQL queries on Amazon RDS. The company needs sessions to survive instance replacement and the leaderboard to update in real time with sub-millisecond reads, with high availability across Availability Zones. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable sticky sessions on the load balancer, and add RDS read replicas to serve the leaderboard queries",
    "Store sessions and leaderboard data in Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached spread across several nodes",
    "Store sessions and leaderboard data in Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey with Multi-AZ, using sorted sets for rankings",
    "Store sessions on a shared Amazon EFS file system and cache the leaderboard query results in CloudFront"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Sticky sessions still lose a player's session when that instance is terminated, and read replicas still run the same expensive SQL at millisecond latencies.",
    "A node-based Memcached cluster does not replicate data, so losing a node drops the sessions stored on it, and Memcached has no sorted-set data type for rankings.",
    "ElastiCache for Valkey (or Redis OSS) keeps sessions off the instances with Multi-AZ replication, and sorted sets maintain a ranked leaderboard in memory with sub-millisecond reads.",
    "EFS adds file-system latency to every session read, and caching leaderboard results in CloudFront serves stale rankings instead of real-time updates."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"m1-032", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["EBS & instance store","KMS & encryption"],
 q:"After an audit, a company must ensure that every new Amazon EBS volume created in its production account in eu-west-1 is encrypted with a specific customer managed KMS key. This includes volumes created by Auto Scaling groups and by teams that forget to select encryption. The company does not want to edit its many existing launch templates and AWS CloudFormation templates. Which solution meets these requirements with the least effort?",
 o:["Deploy the AWS Config managed rule for encrypted volumes and alert the security team about every noncompliant volume",
    "Enable EBS encryption by default for the account in eu-west-1 and set the customer managed key as the default key",
    "Update every launch template and CloudFormation template to set the Encrypted property and the customer managed key ID",
    "Attach an SCP that denies <code>ec2:CreateVolume</code> and <code>ec2:RunInstances</code> requests that do not request encrypted volumes"],
 a:[1],
 x:["The Config rule only reports unencrypted volumes after they exist; it does not encrypt anything.",
    "With encryption by default, every new volume in that Region is encrypted with the chosen key automatically, even when templates and users do not ask for encryption.",
    "Editing every template would work but is exactly the effort the company wants to avoid, and new templates could still forget the setting.",
    "An SCP would block unencrypted launches instead of encrypting them, so every existing template that omits encryption would start failing until it is edited."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"m1-033", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["DynamoDB","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A ticketing company stores orders in an Amazon DynamoDB table. A faulty deployment ran for 40 minutes before it was rolled back, and during that time it overwrote thousands of items with bad data. The company must be able to recover the table to its exact state at any second before such an incident, going back up to 35 days, without building its own backup jobs. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create on-demand backups of the table every hour with an EventBridge Scheduler schedule and a Lambda function",
    "Turn on point-in-time recovery for the table and restore to a new table at the second before the incident starts",
    "Convert the table to a global table with a replica in a second Region and fail over to the replica after an incident",
    "Enable DynamoDB Streams and replay the stream records in reverse order to undo the changes made by the deployment"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Hourly backups could lose up to an hour of valid orders, and the schedule and function are custom jobs to maintain.",
    "Point-in-time recovery keeps continuous backups and can restore the table to any second within the recovery window of up to 35 days, into a new table, with nothing to schedule or operate.",
    "Global tables replicate every write within seconds, so the bad data would already be in the replica; replication protects against Regional failure, not bad writes.",
    "Streams keep records for only 24 hours, and reversing changes by replaying them is complex custom code that is easy to get wrong."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c4"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"m1-034", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A bank's customer-facing API uses Amazon API Gateway and Lambda functions written in Java. Most requests are fast, but when traffic ramps up each morning some users wait several seconds because new execution environments must start the JVM and load large frameworks. The bank requires consistently low latency during business hours (8 AM to 6 PM) and wants to keep using Lambda without paying for pre-initialized capacity overnight. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Set reserved concurrency on the functions to the expected business-hours peak",
    "Create an EventBridge rule that invokes each function every 5 minutes to keep it warm",
    "Increase the functions' memory to the maximum and their timeout to 15 minutes",
    "Configure provisioned concurrency on an alias and schedule it with Application Auto Scaling"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Reserved concurrency only guarantees and caps how many environments a function may use; it does not pre-initialize them, so cold starts still occur.",
    "A periodic ping keeps roughly one environment warm, not the many needed during a morning ramp, so most users would still hit cold starts.",
    "More memory also gives more CPU and can shorten initialization somewhat, but cold starts still happen, and the timeout has nothing to do with startup latency.",
    "Provisioned concurrency keeps environments initialized before requests arrive, and scheduled scaling raises it for business hours and lowers it overnight to control cost."],
 ref:["d10e3"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"m1-035", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["API Gateway","IAM policies"],
 q:"A company exposes a REST API through Amazon API Gateway to partner systems. Partners authenticate with opaque bearer tokens issued by the company's in-house token service, which is not OAuth 2.0 or OpenID Connect compliant and cannot be changed. Each request must be validated with the token service, the API methods a partner may call depend on its subscription, and validation results should be cached briefly to limit calls to the token service. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create an Amazon Cognito user pool, import the partners as users, and add a Cognito user pool authorizer to the API",
    "Require IAM authorization on every method and give each partner an IAM user whose access keys sign the requests",
    "Create a Lambda authorizer that validates the token with the token service and returns a policy, with caching on",
    "Attach an AWS WAF web ACL to the API stage with a rule that allows only requests that carry an Authorization header"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A Cognito authorizer validates tokens that Cognito issued; it cannot validate the in-house tokens that partners must keep using.",
    "IAM authorization would force partners to switch to SigV4 signing with long-term access keys and abandon the existing tokens.",
    "A Lambda authorizer can run custom validation logic, call the token service, and return an IAM policy that allows only the partner's permitted methods; API Gateway caches the result for a configurable TTL.",
    "WAF can check that a header exists or matches a pattern, but it cannot validate a token with the token service or vary permissions per partner."],
 ref:["d8e2","d10b2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"m1-036", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["API Gateway","Lambda"],
 q:"A startup's mobile back end uses an Amazon API Gateway REST API with Lambda proxy integrations and handles about 2 billion requests per month. Requests are authorized with JSON Web Tokens from an OpenID Connect provider. The API uses no caching, usage plans, API keys, request validation, or AWS WAF. API Gateway charges are now one of the largest items on the bill. Which change will reduce cost the most with minimal changes to the clients?",
 o:["Migrate the API to an API Gateway HTTP API with a JWT authorizer and Lambda proxy integrations",
    "Enable API Gateway caching on the REST API stage so that fewer requests reach the Lambda functions",
    "Purchase a Compute Savings Plan that covers the steady usage of the Lambda functions behind the API",
    "Change the REST API endpoint type from edge-optimized to Regional and add a CloudFront distribution"],
 a:[0],
 x:["HTTP APIs cost substantially less per request than REST APIs and support JWT authorizers and Lambda proxy integrations natively, and the REST-only features are not used.",
    "API Gateway still bills every request, and the cache adds an hourly charge, so the API Gateway bill goes up rather than down.",
    "A Savings Plan lowers Lambda compute charges, not API Gateway request charges, which are the item the company needs to reduce.",
    "The endpoint type does not change the per-request price, and an added CloudFront distribution brings its own request and data transfer charges."],
 ref:["d11e3","d10b2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"m1-037", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Elastic Beanstalk"],
 q:"A company runs its customer portal on AWS Elastic Beanstalk, with an Amazon RDS database that is managed outside the environment. Each release must be tested in a full production-like environment before any user traffic reaches it, the cutover must cause no downtime, and if a problem appears after cutover, users must be sent back to the previous version quickly, so that version must keep running until the release is confirmed. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Deploy the new version to the existing environment with the All at once deployment policy",
    "Deploy the new version to the existing environment with the Rolling with additional batch policy",
    "Clone the production environment and deploy the new version to the clone for testing",
    "Use the Swap environment URLs action to move traffic to the tested environment",
    "Use the Immutable deployment policy so that new instances replace the old ones after health checks"],
 a:[2,3],
 x:["All at once updates every instance in place, which causes downtime and leaves no previous version running to roll back to.",
    "Rolling with additional batch keeps full capacity, but users reach the new version batch by batch without separate testing, and rolling back means another deployment.",
    "A separate green environment running the new version can be tested fully while the blue environment keeps serving users, and the database outside the environments is not affected by either one.",
    "Swapping the environments' CNAMEs moves users to the tested environment without downtime, and swapping back returns them to the previous version, which is still running.",
    "Immutable deployments launch new instances inside the same environment and terminate the old ones after success, so users reach the new version without separate testing and no previous environment remains to switch back to."],
 ref:["d9e2","d1w3"], doc:"beanstalk"},

{id:"m1-038", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Threat detection"],
 q:"A security team receives Amazon GuardDuty findings that an IAM role made unusual API calls from an unfamiliar IP address. For each finding, analysts must quickly determine the root cause: which other resources and IP addresses the role interacted with, what it did before and after, and how this compares with its normal behavior over recent months. Today analysts search CloudTrail logs and VPC Flow Logs by hand, which takes hours. Which AWS service should they use to speed up these investigations with the least effort?",
 o:["Amazon Inspector, to scan the role's workloads for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure",
    "Amazon Detective, to analyze and visualize the related activity in its behavior graph over time",
    "Amazon Macie, to discover which S3 objects that the role accessed contain sensitive data",
    "AWS Config, to show the configuration timeline of the role and of its attached IAM policies"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Inspector finds vulnerabilities in EC2, ECR, and Lambda workloads; it does not analyze API activity or reconstruct what a principal did.",
    "Detective automatically builds a behavior graph from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and GuardDuty findings, so analysts can pivot from a finding to related entities and compare activity with historical baselines.",
    "Macie classifies sensitive data in S3; it does not trace a role's API calls, IP addresses, or behavior over time.",
    "Config records configuration changes to resources, not the API activity and network connections needed to investigate the finding."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4c3"]},

{id:"m1-039", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"An IoT company streams JSON telemetry from 50,000 sensors and wants to store it in Amazon S3 for analysis with Amazon Athena. Before storage, a vendor-specific field in each record must be normalized and invalid records must be dropped, and the data must arrive in S3 in compressed batches rather than as millions of tiny objects. The company wants a fully managed pipeline with no servers or consumer applications to operate. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Send the data to Kinesis Data Streams and run KCL consumer applications on EC2 that transform the records and write them to S3",
    "Have the sensors write each record directly to S3 and use S3 event notifications to invoke a Lambda function that rewrites it",
    "Send the data to Amazon Data Firehose with a Lambda transformation function and deliver compressed, buffered batches to S3",
    "Send the data to an Amazon SQS queue and use an AWS Glue crawler on the queue to transform and load the records into S3"],
 a:[2],
 x:["This works, but it requires running and scaling consumer applications on EC2, which the company wants to avoid.",
    "Writing every record as its own object creates millions of tiny files that slow Athena down, and each record is then written twice.",
    "Firehose is serverless, buffers records by size or time, invokes Lambda to transform or drop records, and compresses each batch before delivering it to S3.",
    "Glue crawlers infer schemas from data stores; they cannot read from an SQS queue or transform records in flight."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"], doc:"firehose"},

{id:"m1-040", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["AWS Backup","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A company runs workloads on Amazon EC2 with EBS volumes, Amazon RDS, Amazon EFS, and Amazon DynamoDB across 12 accounts in AWS Organizations. A new policy requires daily backups retained for 35 days, a copy of every backup in a second Region for disaster recovery, and central reporting that proves backups comply with the policy. The solution must minimize custom scripting. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Write Lambda functions for each service that create snapshots and copy them to the second Region, triggered by EventBridge Scheduler",
    "Enable RDS automated backups, Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies for EBS, and point-in-time recovery for DynamoDB in each account",
    "Export all of the data to Amazon S3 every night and use S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy the exports to the second Region",
    "Use AWS Backup plans deployed through Organizations backup policies, with a cross-Region copy rule, and AWS Backup Audit Manager reports"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Custom functions per service can do this, but they are the custom scripting the company wants to avoid, and they provide no compliance reporting.",
    "Separate per-service features must be configured in every account, give no single cross-Region copy policy for all resources, and offer no central compliance reporting.",
    "Exporting every service's data nightly needs custom tooling, and the exports are not restorable backups of the original resources.",
    "AWS Backup covers EC2, EBS, RDS, EFS, and DynamoDB; Organizations backup policies apply plans to every account, a copy rule sends backups to the second Region, and AWS Backup Audit Manager (a feature of AWS Backup, not the separate AWS Audit Manager service) reports compliance."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"backup"},

{id:"m1-041", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["API Gateway","VPC endpoints & PrivateLink"],
 q:"A bank is building internal microservices behind Amazon API Gateway REST APIs. The APIs must be reachable only from the bank's VPCs and from its on-premises data center, which connects to the VPCs over AWS Direct Connect, and must never be reachable from the public internet. API calls must also be accepted only when they arrive through the bank's approved VPC endpoint. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Set the API endpoint type to edge-optimized and associate an AWS WAF web ACL that allows only the bank's IP ranges",
    "Create an interface VPC endpoint for the execute-api service in the VPC that hosts the API consumers",
    "Set the API endpoint type to private and attach a resource policy that allows invocation only through the approved endpoint",
    "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for API Gateway and add it to the route tables of the consumer subnets",
    "Set the API endpoint type to Regional and add a Lambda authorizer that checks the caller's source IP address"],
 a:[1,2],
 x:["An edge-optimized API still has a public endpoint; WAF filters requests but does not make the API private.",
    "Private APIs are reached through an interface endpoint for execute-api, which on-premises clients can also use over Direct Connect.",
    "The private endpoint type removes public reachability, and a resource policy with an <code>aws:SourceVpce</code> condition accepts calls only through the approved endpoint.",
    "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB; API Gateway is reached through interface endpoints.",
    "A Regional API remains publicly reachable, and checking IP addresses in custom code does not satisfy the never-public requirement."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2c3"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"m1-042", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["Aurora"],
 q:"A software company has 30 development and test databases on provisioned Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL instances. Each database sees short, unpredictable bursts of heavy activity while developers run tests and is otherwise idle, including nights and weekends. The databases must be available whenever a developer needs one, without anyone starting them manually, and the company wants to stop paying for idle provisioned capacity. Which solution is the most cost-effective?",
 o:["Purchase 1-year Reserved Instances for all 30 provisioned Aurora instances",
    "Convert the databases to Aurora Serverless v2 with a low minimum capacity setting",
    "Stop all 30 Aurora clusters every evening and start them each morning on a schedule",
    "Consolidate all 30 databases onto one large provisioned Aurora cluster for the team"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Reserved Instances lower the hourly rate but still pay for every instance around the clock, including the long idle periods.",
    "Aurora Serverless v2 scales up within seconds during test bursts and back down when idle; on supported engine versions the minimum can be 0 ACUs, so idle databases pause and resume automatically when a developer connects.",
    "Usage is unpredictable and databases must be available on demand, so a fixed schedule would block developers outside the window and still pay for idle time inside it.",
    "One large cluster must be sized for the combined peaks around the clock and creates noisy-neighbor problems between teams."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"m1-043", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A logistics company keeps shipment events as Parquet files in Amazon S3, current inventory in Amazon DynamoDB, and customer accounts in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Analysts occasionally need ad hoc SQL queries that join all three sources with current data. The company does not want to build or schedule pipelines that copy the DynamoDB and RDS data into S3 first. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Use Amazon Athena federated queries with data source connectors for DynamoDB and PostgreSQL, joined with the S3 tables",
    "Load all three sources into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster each night with COPY commands and AWS DMS tasks",
    "Create AWS Glue ETL jobs that export the DynamoDB table and the RDS tables to S3 every hour, and query the copies with Athena",
    "Replicate the DynamoDB table into the RDS database with DynamoDB Streams and Lambda, and run the joins in PostgreSQL"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Athena federated query uses Lambda-based connectors that read DynamoDB and PostgreSQL in place, so one SQL statement can join them with S3 data without any copy pipeline.",
    "Nightly loads into a provisioned cluster are copy pipelines plus a cluster to run, and the data would be up to a day old.",
    "Scheduled Glue export jobs are exactly the copy pipelines the company wants to avoid, and the copies lag behind the sources.",
    "Streams and Lambda form a custom replication pipeline to build and operate, and the S3 data still could not be joined from PostgreSQL."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"], doc:"athena"},

{id:"m1-044", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A payments company encrypts cardholder fields in its application with AWS KMS before writing them to Amazon DynamoDB global tables in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. If one Region becomes unavailable, the application in the other Region must decrypt records that were encrypted in the failed Region, without calling KMS in the failed Region and without re-encrypting the data. Keys must remain generated and managed by KMS. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a single-Region customer managed key in each Region, give both keys the same alias, and use the local key in each Region",
    "Create a single-Region key in us-east-1 and grant the eu-west-1 application <code>kms:Decrypt</code> permission on that key",
    "Export the key material from a KMS key in us-east-1 and import it into a new customer managed key in eu-west-1",
    "Create a multi-Region primary key in us-east-1 and a replica key in eu-west-1, and use the local key in each Region"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Keys that share an alias still have different key material and key IDs, so ciphertext produced under one cannot be decrypted with the other.",
    "Decryption would still require calls to KMS in us-east-1, which fails when that Region is unavailable.",
    "KMS never exports the key material of keys it generates, so this is not possible.",
    "Multi-Region keys share the same key ID and key material across Regions, so data encrypted under the primary in one Region can be decrypted locally with the replica in the other."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"m1-045", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"An IoT company invokes a Lambda function asynchronously from Amazon S3 event notifications each time a device uploads a log file. Occasionally a malformed file makes the function fail, and after Lambda's built-in retries are exhausted, the event is discarded and cannot be reprocessed. The team wants every failed event captured automatically, together with the error details, so it can be inspected and replayed later, without changing the function code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure an on-failure destination for asynchronous invocations that sends invocation records to an Amazon SQS queue",
    "Raise the function's timeout to 15 minutes and its reserved concurrency so it has enough time and capacity to finish",
    "Lower the asynchronous retry attempts to zero and the maximum event age to 60 seconds so failures surface faster",
    "Turn on CloudWatch Logs for the function and search the logs for failed request IDs whenever events need replaying"],
 a:[0],
 x:["An on-failure destination receives a record for each event that exhausts its retries, including the original payload and the error response, and the queue holds it for inspection and replay (a dead-letter queue is the older alternative).",
    "More time and capacity do not fix a malformed file; the function would still fail and the events would still be discarded.",
    "Changing retry and age settings only makes events fail sooner; without a destination or dead-letter queue they are still discarded.",
    "Logs may not contain the full event payload, and searching them by hand is not automatic capture for replay."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9b1"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"m1-046", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["Threat detection","S3 security"],
 q:"A healthcare startup has accumulated several hundred S3 buckets across its AWS accounts over five years. Before a compliance audit, the security team must find which buckets contain personally identifiable information (PII), such as names, addresses, and national ID numbers, in documents and CSV exports, and must keep evaluating new data automatically. The team wants a managed service instead of writing its own scanning code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection in every account and review the findings it generates for the buckets",
    "Run Amazon Inspector scans against all of the buckets and filter its findings for exposed sensitive data",
    "Enable Amazon Macie for the organization and turn on automated sensitive data discovery for the buckets",
    "Catalog the buckets with AWS Glue crawlers and query them in Amazon Athena with regular expressions for PII"],
 a:[2],
 x:["GuardDuty S3 Protection analyzes data access events for suspicious activity; it does not inspect object contents for PII.",
    "Inspector scans EC2 instances, container images, and Lambda functions for vulnerabilities; it does not scan S3 object contents.",
    "Macie uses managed data identifiers and machine learning to find PII in S3 objects, and automated discovery keeps sampling buckets across the organization as data changes.",
    "This is the custom scanning work the team wants to avoid, and regular expressions in Athena cannot reliably parse documents such as PDFs."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4c3"]},

{id:"m1-047", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["EC2 instance types","Containers"],
 q:"A SaaS company runs 200 x86-based m5 EC2 instances for a Java microservices platform. Services are built by a CI/CD pipeline into container images, and none of them depend on x86-specific binaries. The company wants the largest improvement in compute price-performance without rewriting the application or changing how it buys capacity. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Move the services to burstable t3 instances in unlimited mode to lower the hourly rate",
    "Move the services to the latest generation of x86 instances of the same size and family",
    "Move the services to fewer, larger x86 instances and run more containers on each one",
    "Build arm64 images in the pipeline and move the services to Graviton-based instances"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Sustained load on burstable instances in unlimited mode accrues surplus CPU credit charges, so steady microservices often cost more, not less.",
    "Newer x86 generations do improve price-performance, but Graviton-based instances typically deliver a larger improvement for Java workloads that run on Arm unchanged.",
    "Larger instances cost roughly the same per vCPU, so consolidating mainly reduces the instance count without improving price-performance.",
    "Java runs on Arm without code changes, so building multi-architecture images lets the services move to Graviton instances, which offer the best price-performance on EC2 for this kind of workload."],
 ref:["d11e3"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"m1-048", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases","Caching"],
 q:"A fintech startup needs a primary database for user wallets and session tokens. The application already uses Valkey data structures and commands, needs microsecond read latency and single-digit-millisecond writes, and cannot lose any acknowledged write if a node or an Availability Zone fails. The company wants a fully managed service. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey with Multi-AZ replicas and automatic failover enabled",
    "Amazon MemoryDB with a primary and replicas spread across several Availability Zones",
    "Amazon DynamoDB with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) in front of the wallet table",
    "Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with Aurora Replicas and a large in-memory buffer cache"],
 a:[1],
 x:["ElastiCache replicates asynchronously, so writes acknowledged just before a primary failure can be lost when a replica is promoted; it is designed as a cache, not a durable primary database.",
    "MemoryDB is a durable in-memory database compatible with Valkey and Redis OSS: writes are committed to a Multi-AZ transaction log before they are acknowledged, while reads are served from memory at microsecond latency.",
    "DAX gives microsecond reads, but the application would have to be rewritten from Valkey commands to the DynamoDB API.",
    "Aurora is durable, but it is a relational database with millisecond latency that does not support Valkey commands and data structures."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3c4"]},

{id:"m1-049", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery","RDS"],
 q:"A regional bank's internal loan-processing application runs on EC2 instances with an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database in us-east-2. For a Regional disaster, the bank accepts an RTO of 4 hours but requires an RPO of under 1 minute because loan data cannot be re-entered. The application servers can be deployed from AMIs with AWS CloudFormation in about 30 minutes. The bank wants the lowest-cost strategy that meets both objectives. Which disaster recovery approach should it use?",
 o:["Backup and restore: take hourly RDS snapshots and copy them to a second Region with AWS Backup",
    "Warm standby: run a scaled-down application fleet and a database replica in a second Region at all times",
    "Pilot light: keep a cross-Region read replica running and deploy the application tier only during a disaster",
    "Multi-site active/active: run the full application and database capacity in both Regions continuously"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Snapshots copied hourly can lose up to an hour of data, which fails the under-1-minute RPO.",
    "Warm standby meets the objectives but pays for an always-on application fleet that a 4-hour RTO does not require.",
    "Continuous asynchronous replication keeps the RPO in seconds, and deploying the application tier in about 30 minutes fits easily within the 4-hour RTO, with only the database running in the second Region.",
    "Active/active gives a near-zero RTO, but paying for full capacity in two Regions is far more than the objectives justify."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"]},

{id:"m1-050", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager","CloudFront"],
 q:"An online travel agency serves its fare search pages through Amazon CloudFront. Competitors run automated scrapers that copy fares all day. The scrapers rotate through thousands of IP addresses, stay under any single-IP request rate, and imitate browser user agents. The company must block these bots while still allowing verified search engine crawlers to index the site, with the least custom development. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add the AWS WAF Bot Control managed rule group at the targeted inspection level to the distribution's web ACL",
    "Add an AWS WAF rate-based rule to the distribution's web ACL that blocks IP addresses above a low request rate",
    "Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced and enable automatic application layer DDoS mitigation for the distribution",
    "Configure CloudFront geographic restrictions that block the countries where most of the scraper traffic originates"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Bot Control identifies verified bots such as search engine crawlers so that they can be allowed, and its targeted level uses browser interrogation, fingerprinting, and behavior analysis to catch sophisticated bots that rotate IP addresses.",
    "Each scraper IP address stays below any per-IP rate, so a rate-based rule rarely triggers, and a very low limit would also block real users and crawlers.",
    "Shield Advanced mitigates DDoS attacks; low-rate scraping that looks like normal traffic is not a DDoS event, so it would not be blocked.",
    "Geographic restrictions block whole countries, including real customers, and scrapers can simply use IP addresses in allowed countries."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4c3","d8c3"], doc:"waf"},

{id:"m1-051", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Redshift","Analytics"],
 q:"A retail company's BI dashboards query an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. Each dashboard runs the same complex aggregation queries over billions of sales rows every few minutes, and the results change only when the hourly load finishes. At 9 AM hundreds of users open dashboards at once and queries wait in queues for minutes, while the rest of the day the cluster is lightly used. Which combination of actions will improve dashboard performance most cost-effectively? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Double the number of nodes in the cluster permanently so that it can absorb the 9 AM peak",
    "Unload the sales table to S3 every hour and point the dashboards at Amazon Athena instead",
    "Create materialized views for the dashboard aggregations and have Redshift refresh them after loads",
    "Change the sales table's distribution style to ALL so that every node holds a full copy of it",
    "Turn on concurrency scaling for the queues that the dashboard queries use during the morning peak"],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["A permanently larger cluster handles the peak but pays for idle capacity for the rest of the day, so it is not cost-effective.",
    "Athena would rescan the data in S3 for every dashboard query and the hourly unload is a new pipeline; nothing is precomputed.",
    "Materialized views store precomputed aggregation results, so repeated dashboard queries read small result sets instead of scanning billions of rows, and Redshift can refresh them automatically.",
    "Distribution style ALL suits small dimension tables; copying a billion-row fact table to every node wastes storage and slows loads.",
    "Concurrency scaling adds transient capacity when queries start to queue and releases it afterward, and the cluster earns free credits for it, so the company pays little or nothing for a short daily peak."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c3"], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"m1-052", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["VPC endpoints & PrivateLink"],
 q:"A bank's workloads run in private subnets with no NAT gateway or internet gateway and reach Amazon SQS through an interface VPC endpoint. A security review found that an insider could use credentials from a personal AWS account on these instances to send internal data through the endpoint to a queue in that personal account. The bank must block access through the endpoint to any queue that its own accounts do not own, without changing queue policies or application IAM roles. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Restrict the endpoint's security group so that it allows inbound HTTPS only from the application subnets' CIDR ranges",
    "Attach an SCP to the bank's accounts that denies SQS actions on any queue that is outside the bank's organization",
    "Enable private DNS on the endpoint so that SQS requests from the VPC resolve only to queues in the bank's accounts",
    "Attach a VPC endpoint policy to the SQS endpoint that allows SQS actions only on queues in the bank's AWS accounts"],
 a:[3],
 x:["The security group controls which clients can reach the endpoint, not which queues they can send to once connected.",
    "SCPs apply only to principals in the bank's accounts; the insider would be using credentials from a personal account, which the bank's SCPs do not govern.",
    "Private DNS only makes the standard SQS hostname resolve to the endpoint; it does not restrict which accounts' queues can be reached.",
    "An endpoint policy is evaluated for every request through the endpoint, whoever's credentials are used, so limiting its Resource element to queue ARNs in the bank's accounts closes the exfiltration path."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2c3","d8b1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"m1-053", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"An insurance company must keep scanned claim documents for 10 years after upload and then delete them automatically. Documents are accessed often during the first 60 days and rarely for the rest of the first year. After that they are retrieved only for legal requests, which allow up to 48 hours to produce a document. Which S3 storage strategy is the most cost-effective?",
 o:["S3 Standard, then S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 60 days, then expiration after 10 years",
    "S3 Standard, then Standard-IA after 60 days, then Glacier Deep Archive after 1 year, then expiration after 10 years",
    "S3 Standard, then Standard-IA after 60 days, then Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 1 year, then expiration after 10 years",
    "S3 Intelligent-Tiering from upload, with a scheduled Lambda function that deletes documents older than 10 years"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Glacier Instant Retrieval suits rarely accessed data that needs millisecond access; keeping documents there for 9 more years costs much more than Deep Archive when 48 hours is acceptable.",
    "Each tier matches the access pattern: Standard-IA for rare access in the first year, then Deep Archive, the lowest-cost class, whose standard retrievals finish within 12 hours, with lifecycle expiration deleting documents at 10 years.",
    "Flexible Retrieval also meets the 48-hour need, but it costs noticeably more per GB than Deep Archive across 9 years of archive storage.",
    "Without the opt-in archive tiers, Intelligent-Tiering never goes below its instant-access tiers, adds a per-object monitoring fee, and a custom function replaces a simple lifecycle expiration."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3b2","d3c3"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"m1-054", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"A company has a separate AWS account for each business unit. Each account sends custom application events and AWS service events, such as EC2 instance state changes, to its default Amazon EventBridge event bus. A central operations account must receive selected events from all business unit accounts and route them to several targets, including an SQS queue and a Step Functions state machine. The solution must not use custom forwarding or polling code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["In each business unit account, add rules that invoke a Lambda function that calls PutEvents on the operations account's event bus",
    "Create an SNS topic in each business unit account, and subscribe the operations account's SQS queue to every one of the topics",
    "Create an event bus in the operations account whose resource policy allows the organization, and add rules in each account that target it",
    "Enable an organization trail in CloudTrail, and have the operations account parse the delivered S3 log files for the needed events"],
 a:[2],
 x:["This forwards events with custom code, which the requirement rules out, and it is unnecessary because EventBridge can target another account's bus directly.",
    "SNS does not receive EventBridge service events without extra rules, and the state machine would need custom code to poll the queue.",
    "EventBridge rules can send events directly to an event bus in another account; the central bus's resource policy allows the organization to put events, and rules on that bus route them to SQS and Step Functions.",
    "CloudTrail delivers API activity to S3 in batches, not custom application events in near real time, and parsing log files is custom code."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9b3"], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"m1-055", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["S3 features"],
 q:"A film production company has crews in Australia, Brazil, and South Africa that upload raw footage files of 20–100 GB each to a single S3 bucket in us-east-1 over the public internet. Uploads are slow and inconsistent because of the long distances. The upload tool already uses multipart upload. The company wants faster uploads with minimal changes to the tool and without deploying or managing resources in other Regions. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create buckets in Regions near each crew and replicate them to the us-east-1 bucket with S3 Cross-Region Replication",
    "Set up AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections from each crew location to a VPC in us-east-1 with an S3 gateway endpoint",
    "Increase the multipart upload part size to the 5 GB maximum so that each file is sent in fewer, larger parts",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and configure the upload tool to use the accelerate endpoint"],
 a:[3],
 x:["This means creating and managing buckets and replication in three other Regions, which the company wants to avoid, and it adds replication costs.",
    "VPN traffic still crosses the public internet over the same long paths with limited tunnel throughput, and gateway endpoints do not accept traffic arriving over a VPN.",
    "Larger parts reduce the number of requests but do nothing about the long, congested internet path that limits throughput; many smaller parts uploaded in parallel are usually faster.",
    "Transfer Acceleration routes uploads into the nearest edge location and across the AWS backbone to the bucket, and the tool only needs to switch to the accelerate endpoint."],
 ref:["d10e5","d10e2"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"m1-056", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager","Internet & NAT gateways"],
 q:"A company's workloads in private subnets across many VPCs reach the internet through NAT gateways in a central egress VPC attached to a transit gateway. A new security policy requires outbound HTTPS to be allowed only to an approved list of domain names, such as software repositories and partner APIs, with all other outbound traffic blocked and logged. The solution must be a managed service that scales automatically. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Update the security groups of all workloads to allow outbound port 443 only to the current IP addresses of the approved domains",
    "Deploy AWS Network Firewall in the egress VPC, route traffic through its endpoints before the NAT gateways, and use a domain allow list",
    "Add network ACL rules to the egress VPC subnets that allow outbound traffic only to the approved domain names",
    "Associate an AWS WAF web ACL with the NAT gateways that allows only requests whose Host header matches an approved domain"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Security groups cannot match domain names, and the IP addresses behind repositories and APIs change often, so the rules would break and need constant updates.",
    "Network Firewall is a managed, auto-scaling firewall whose stateful domain list rules match TLS SNI and HTTP Host values, allowing only approved domains and logging the rest; inspecting in the egress VPC covers every spoke VPC.",
    "Network ACLs match only CIDR ranges, ports, and protocols; they cannot filter by domain name.",
    "AWS WAF attaches to resources such as CloudFront, ALB, and API Gateway, not to NAT gateways, and it inspects inbound web requests rather than outbound traffic."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2w1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"m1-057", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["Containers","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A company runs a containerized REST API on Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type behind an Application Load Balancer. Traffic ranges from a few requests per second at night to several thousand during unannounced marketing campaigns, and an engineer currently changes the service's desired task count by hand. The company wants the task count to adjust automatically to keep latency stable, with no servers or cluster capacity to manage. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure ECS Service Auto Scaling with a target tracking policy on the ALB request count per target for the service",
    "Create an Auto Scaling group capacity provider with managed scaling and attach it to the ECS cluster for the service",
    "Create EventBridge Scheduler schedules that raise the desired task count before campaigns and lower it afterward",
    "Increase the CPU and memory in the task definition so that each task can handle the highest expected traffic"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Service Auto Scaling adjusts the desired task count through Application Auto Scaling; tracking request count per target adds tasks as traffic rises and removes them as it falls, and Fargate supplies the capacity.",
    "Auto Scaling group capacity providers scale EC2 container instances for the EC2 launch type; with Fargate there are no instances, and this does not change the service's task count.",
    "Schedules only help for planned events; unannounced campaigns would still overload the service.",
    "Oversized tasks pay for peak capacity all the time and still put a fixed ceiling on throughput."],
 ref:["d9e2","d1c8"], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"m1-058", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company has 600 EC2 instances across 15 accounts in AWS Organizations, launched over several years by different teams. Many appear oversized, but nobody knows which ones or what size they should be. The cloud team wants data-driven recommendations for specific instance types and sizes, based on CPU and memory utilization, for all accounts, with minimal effort. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Review CPU utilization graphs in CloudWatch in every account and downsize any instance that averages under 20% CPU",
    "Use AWS Cost Explorer Savings Plans recommendations to find which instances should be moved to a smaller size",
    "Opt in to AWS Compute Optimizer for the organization, publish memory metrics with the CloudWatch agent, and apply its recommendations",
    "Deploy AWS Config rules in every account that flag instances that are not using an instance type from an approved list"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Manual review across 15 accounts is slow, ignores memory, and an average-CPU threshold does not say which instance type or size to choose.",
    "Savings Plans recommendations suggest a commitment amount based on past usage; they do not recommend instance types or sizes.",
    "Compute Optimizer analyzes utilization history across all member accounts and recommends specific instance types and sizes, and with the CloudWatch agent publishing memory metrics its recommendations also account for memory.",
    "Config checks configuration against rules; it has no utilization data, so it cannot tell whether an instance is oversized."],
 ref:["d11e3"]},

{id:"m1-059", day:"mock", dom:1, tags:["S3 security"],
 q:"A photo-printing website lets customers download high-resolution images of their own orders. The images are stored in an S3 bucket that must stay private. The site's backend runs on Lambda behind Amazon API Gateway and already authenticates customers. The company wants each customer to download only their own image directly from S3 for 15 minutes after clicking a link, without streaming large files through Lambda and without creating AWS identities for customers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Have the Lambda function generate a presigned GET URL for the customer's object that expires after 15 minutes",
    "Grant public read on the customer's object with an object ACL, and remove the ACL after 15 minutes with a scheduled function",
    "Create an IAM user for each customer with a policy that allows <code>s3:GetObject</code> on that customer's order prefix",
    "Turn on static website hosting and add a bucket policy that allows requests that come from API Gateway's IP addresses"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A presigned URL carries temporary authorization from the function's role for one object, so the customer downloads directly from S3 until it expires while the bucket stays private.",
    "Public ACLs break the private-bucket requirement, are disabled by default on new buckets, and anyone with the link could download the image during that window.",
    "Creating IAM users for customers is the opposite of what is required and does not scale to a consumer website.",
    "Website endpoints serve only public content, and the download request would come from the customer's browser, not from API Gateway."],
 ref:["d8e3","d3b1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"m1-060", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["Aurora"],
 q:"A news aggregation service uses an Amazon Aurora MySQL provisioned cluster with one writer and two Aurora Replicas. When major stories break, read traffic can grow tenfold within 15 minutes, replica CPU reaches 95%, and page loads slow down; writes stay steady. The company wants read capacity to grow and shrink automatically with demand while keeping the provisioned cluster and its current approach of sending reads to the reader endpoint. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Modify the writer instance to a larger instance class so that it can absorb more of the traffic during spikes",
    "Create cross-Region Aurora read replicas in two more Regions and send part of the read traffic to each of them",
    "Configure Aurora Auto Scaling with a target tracking policy on the average CPU utilization of the Aurora Replicas",
    "Place Amazon RDS Proxy in front of the cluster to pool connections and spread the reads more efficiently"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Reads go to the replicas through the reader endpoint, so a bigger writer adds no read capacity, and it would be sized for spikes all the time.",
    "Cross-Region replicas are not added or removed automatically with load, add replication cost, and would need application changes to use.",
    "Aurora Auto Scaling adds Aurora Replicas when average replica CPU passes the target and removes them when demand falls, and the reader endpoint spreads connections across the new replicas automatically.",
    "RDS Proxy manages connections but adds no compute capacity, so CPU-bound replicas would stay overloaded."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"m1-061", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["S3 features","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A media company stores newly produced video assets in an S3 bucket in us-east-1. For business continuity, every new object must also exist in a bucket in us-west-2. A contract with a broadcast partner requires that 99.99% of new objects be replicated within 15 minutes, backed by an SLA, and the operations team must be alerted when replication falls behind. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication and add a CloudWatch alarm on the object count of the destination bucket",
    "Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication with S3 Replication Time Control, replication metrics, and event notifications",
    "Schedule an S3 Batch Replication job every 15 minutes with EventBridge Scheduler to copy any new objects",
    "Create an AWS DataSync task between the two buckets and run it on a frequent schedule throughout the day"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Standard replication copies most objects quickly but has no 15-minute guarantee or SLA, and the daily S3 storage metrics cannot detect lag in time.",
    "Replication Time Control is designed to replicate 99.99% of new objects within 15 minutes, is backed by an SLA, and provides replication metrics and events for objects that miss the threshold.",
    "Batch Replication is designed for existing or previously failed objects; running it on a schedule adds cost and operational work and carries no SLA.",
    "DataSync runs scheduled transfer tasks with no replication SLA, so it cannot guarantee the 15-minute requirement."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"m1-062", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company runs workloads for six product teams in one shared AWS account, and all resources already carry a Team tag. Finance wants each team's monthly costs broken out in billing reports, and each team lead must receive an email when the team's forecasted monthly spend is expected to exceed its budget. Which combination of actions meets these requirements with the least effort? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Activate the Team tag as a user-defined cost allocation tag in the Billing and Cost Management console",
    "Create a CloudWatch billing alarm for each team that uses the Team tag as a dimension of the charges metric",
    "Move each team's resources into its own AWS account and use consolidated billing to report each team's costs",
    "Turn on AWS Cost Anomaly Detection and subscribe each team lead to the anomaly alerts for the whole account",
    "Create an AWS Budgets cost budget for each team, filtered on its Team tag value, with a forecasted-spend alert"],
 a:[0,4],
 x:["Tags appear in cost reports and can be used to filter budgets only after they are activated as cost allocation tags.",
    "The EstimatedCharges billing metric is broken down by service and linked account, not by resource tags, so it cannot alert per team.",
    "Separate accounts would allocate costs cleanly but require migrating every workload, far more effort than using the existing tags.",
    "Anomaly Detection flags unusual spending patterns; it does not alert against each team's budget or forecast, and account-wide alerts are not per team.",
    "Budgets can filter costs by an activated tag value and email an alert when forecasted spend is expected to exceed the budgeted amount."],
 ref:["d11e3","d1d1","d6w3"], doc:"budgets"},

{id:"m1-063", day:"mock", dom:3, tags:["EFS"],
 q:"A media company runs a content management system on Linux EC2 instances that share an Amazon EFS file system in Bursting throughput mode. The file system stores only 200 GB, but nightly batch renders drive throughput far above the baseline, burst credits run out, and jobs slow to a crawl. Throughput needs are spiky and unpredictable, and the company does not want to pay for throughput it does not use. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Switch the file system to Elastic throughput mode",
    "Switch the file system to Provisioned throughput mode set to the nightly peak",
    "Add about 2 TB of filler files to raise the file system's baseline throughput",
    "Replace EFS with a gp3 EBS volume attached to all instances with Multi-Attach"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Elastic throughput scales up and down automatically with the workload's demand and bills for the throughput actually used, which suits spiky, unpredictable patterns.",
    "Provisioned throughput removes the credit problem but charges for the peak level around the clock, even though the peak lasts only a few hours.",
    "The bursting baseline grows with the amount stored, so this would raise throughput, but it means paying for storage that holds nothing useful.",
    "Multi-Attach is supported only on Provisioned IOPS (io1 and io2) volumes, and a shared block device is not a shared file system without a cluster-aware file system."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3p3"], doc:"efs"},

{id:"m1-064", day:"mock", dom:2, tags:["SQS","High availability design"],
 q:"A retailer's checkout service writes each order synchronously to an Amazon RDS database. During a maintenance window and a separate unexpected outage, customers received errors and some orders were lost. The company wants checkout to keep accepting orders even when the database is unavailable for up to several hours, and every order must be saved after the database comes back. Order processing does not need to be synchronous with checkout. Which change best meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable Multi-AZ on the database so that maintenance and failures no longer interrupt order writes",
    "Add a read replica and have checkout write orders to the replica whenever the primary is unavailable",
    "Send orders to an Amazon SQS queue and have a separate worker write them to the database with retries",
    "Place Amazon ElastiCache in front of the database and write orders to the cache when the database is down"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Multi-AZ shortens failovers to a minute or two, but checkout would still fail during each failover, and it does not help when the database is unavailable for hours.",
    "Read replicas are read-only, so checkout cannot write orders to them.",
    "SQS durably stores orders (for up to 14 days) while the database is unavailable, so checkout keeps succeeding, and the worker writes each order once the database returns.",
    "A cache is not a durable write queue; cached orders can be evicted or lost on node failure, and nothing drains them into the database."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9b1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"m1-065", day:"mock", dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools","EventBridge"],
 q:"A company's development environment consists of EC2 instances and Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instances that developers use only on weekdays from 8 AM to 6 PM, but everything currently runs 24/7. The company wants to cut the cost of this environment with the least operational effort, keeping all data and configuration intact between working days. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Purchase 1-year Reserved Instances for the development EC2 instances and RDS DB instances",
    "Terminate the instances every evening and recreate them each morning from AMIs and snapshots",
    "Convert the development EC2 instances to Spot Instances and keep the RDS DB instances running",
    "Use EventBridge Scheduler to stop the EC2 and RDS instances each evening and start them each weekday morning"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Reserved Instances discount the hourly rate but still bill for all 168 hours a week, while the environment is used for only 50.",
    "Rebuilding every day is far more work and risk than stopping and starting, and it is easy to lose data or configuration in the process.",
    "Spot capacity can interrupt developers during the day and does nothing for the RDS instances that keep running overnight.",
    "Scheduler's universal targets call the EC2 and RDS stop and start APIs directly on a cron schedule; stopped instances incur no compute charges (storage still bills), and data and configuration stay intact."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11e4","d9b3"]}
);
